      "first_s": 0.6565122910001264,
      "text": "```python\ndef median(numbers: list) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the median of a nonempty list of numbers.\n    \n    For an odd-length list, the median is the middle element.\n    For an even-length list, the median is the average of the two middle elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        raise ValueError(\"List must be nonempty\")\n    \n    sorted_nums = sorted(numbers)\n    n = len(sorted_nums)\n    mid = n // 2\n    \n    if n % 2 == 1:\n        return float(sorted_nums[mid])\n    else:\n        return (sorted_nums[mid - 1] + sorted_nums[mid]) / 2.0\n\n\n# Assert tests\nassert median([1, 3, 5]) == 3.0\nassert median([1, 2, 3, 4]) == 2.5\n```\n\n**Explanation (100 words):**\n\nFor an even-length list, the function first sorts the numbers. It calculates the middle index using integer division (`n // 2`). Since the list has an even number of elements, there are two central values: one at index `mid - 1` and the other at index `mid`. The function retrieves both values, adds them together, and divides by 2.0 to compute their arithmetic mean. This average is returned as the median. This approach ensures the median accurately represents the central tendency of the dataset, even when no single element sits exactly in the middle.<|im_end|>",
